o prevent marine pollution from ships."}, {"sourceSlug": "global-maritime-distress-and-safety-system", "sourceName": "Global Maritime Distress and Safety System (GMDSS)", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "international-maritime-organization", "targetName": "International Maritime Organization", "context": "The GMDSS, developed under the auspices of the IMO, represents a significant advancement in maritime communication and safety."}, {"sourceSlug": "climate-change-initiatives", "sourceName": "Climate Change Initiatives", "verb": "TRANSFORMS", "targetSlug": "international-maritime-organization", "targetName": "International Maritime Organization", "context": "The IMO has transformed its focus to include climate change, setting policies for reducing greenhouse gas emissions from shipping."}], "places": [{"name": "London, United Kingdom", "role": "Headquarters"}, {"name": "Geneva, Switzerland", "role": "Founding Location"}, {"name": "New York, United States", "role": "UN Session Venue"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: International Maritime Organization", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "international-maritime-organization"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q201054", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Maritime_Organization"], "tags": ["United Kingdom", "specialized agency of the United Nations", "Europe", "International Organizations", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "International Maritime Organization left a notable mark on Contemporary history, documented across 64 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Shaped institutional landscape and community development. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "The IMO has fundamentally shaped maritime safety and environmental standards, influencing the practices of over 170 countries and significantly reducing maritime accidents, thereby impacting global trade and environmental governance.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-15T14:45:03Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27554080743", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Specialized un agency concerned with legal and regulatory aspects of maritime activity Headquartered in London. Founded 1982.", "newValue": "The International Maritime Organization (IMO) is a specialized agency of the United Nations established on March 6, 1948, in Geneva, Switzerland, primarily to address the safety and security of international shipping and to prevent marine pollution from ships. Headquartered in London since 1965, the IMO plays a crucial role in shaping the regulatory framework for maritime operations, influencing global trade and environmental policies. Its formation was driven by the post-World War II context, where the expansion of international trade necessitated a unified approach to maritime safety and environmental protection.

The organization convenes member states and maritime experts to develop and update conventions, codes, and guidelines aimed at ensuring safe and efficient shipping practices. Key milestones include the adoption of the International Convention for the Safety of Life at Sea (SOLAS) in 1974, which set comprehensive safety standards for merchant vessels, and the International Convention for the Prevention of Pollution from Ships (MARPOL), which established protocols to combat marine pollution. Through these and other regulations, the IMO has significantly influenced shipping practices, reduced maritime accidents, and promoted sustainable environmental stewardship in the oceans.

